		  which is the natural dynamic associated to the usual
		  quantum sine-Gordon model. It is shown that when
		  \$\$\textbraceleft\textbackslash
		  beta\textasciicircum\textbraceleft 2\textbraceright{}
		  \textbackslash in (0, \textbackslash frac\textbraceleft
		  16\textbackslash pi\textbraceright\textbraceleft
		  3\textbraceright )\textbraceright\$\$the Wick renormalised
		  equation is well-posed. In the regime
		  \$\$\textbraceleft\textbackslash
		  beta\textasciicircum\textbraceleft 2\textbraceright{}
		  \textbackslash in (0, 4\textbackslash
		  pi)\textbraceright\$\$, the Da Prato--Debussche method [J
		  Funct Anal 196(1):180--210, 2002; Ann Probab
		  31(4):1900--1916, 2003] applies, while for
		  \$\$\textbraceleft\textbackslash
		  beta\textasciicircum\textbraceleft 2\textbraceright{}
		  \textbackslash in [4\textbackslash pi, \textbackslash
		  frac\textbraceleft 16\textbackslash
		  pi\textbraceright\textbraceleft 3\textbraceright
		  )\textbraceright\$\$, the solution theory is provided via
		  the theory of regularity structures [Hairer, Invent Math
		  198(2):269--504, 2014]. We also show that this model arises
		  naturally from a class of \$\$\textbraceleft 2 +
		  1\textbraceright\$\$-dimensional equilibrium interface
		  fluctuation models with periodic nonlinearities. The main
		  mathematical difficulty arises in the construction of the
		  model for the associated regularity structure where the
		  role of the noise is played by a non-Gaussian random
		  distribution similar to the complex multiplicative Gaussian
		  chaos recently analysed in Lacoin et~al. [Commun Math Phys 337(2):569--632, 2015].},
